3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 11 SEP 1968
2 ADDR
3 CITY Rock Springs
3 STAE Wyoming
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Funeral services were held at the Lyman L. D. S. Ward Chapel in Lyman Saturday, September 14, 1968 at 2:00 p. m. for Mrs. Carrie Kemsley Walker, 83, of Lyman, who died Wednesday in Kimberly Manor, Rock Springs afte
2 CONC r a lingering illness. L. D. S. Bishop Ronald Walker, assisted by Ivan Bluemel, was in charge. Burial was in the Lyman cemetery.
2 CONT Prelude and postlude organ music was played by Cassandra Bluemel.
2 CONT The family prayer was offered by a son, Alfred Walker.
2 CONT The service was opened with prayer by a grandson, Ralph Lowell, followed by a hymn sung by the Lyman Ward Choir, directed by Charles Keller.
2 CONT The obituary with tribute was read by a grandson, Lloyd C. Walker.
2 CONT Talk by a long time friend, Clinton Bradshaw; vocal trio, granddaughters-in-law, Nola Bluemel, Peggy Bluemel and Marjery Taylor, sang “Wonderful Mother of Mine”; talk, Darius M. Syme; the trio of granddaughters-in-law also sang, “
2 CONC In The Garden”; closing prayer by grandson, Rodney Bluemel.
2 CONT Dedication of the grave was given by a grandson-in-law, Alden Maxfield.
2 CONT The beautiful floral tributes we carried by grandchildren and great grandchildren.
2 CONT Pallbearers were grandsons Dale Taylor, Jerry Walker, Dick Walker, Robert Bluemel, James Pearce Jr., and Lenard Bluemel.
2 CONT Lunch was served by the Lyman Ward Relief Society for the family members on return from the cemetery.
2 CONT Mrs. Carrie Kemsley Walker was born August 6, 1885 in Sublette, Idaho, the daughter of Jesse and Elizabeth King Kemsley. She was married to Charles H. Walker September 5, 1906, in Huntsville, Utah.
2 CONT The family has lived in Bridger Valley since 1915, residing on a ranch about 6 miles southeast of Lyman until about twenty years ago when they moved into town.
2 CONT Mrs. Walker was a member of the L. D. S. Church and worked hard in the Primary and Sunday School, while her children were young and then in the relief Society where she completed many tasks and projects.
2 CONT A lover of flowers, she created a flower garden where ever she went. She took up the hobby of braiding rugs at which she was an artist.
2 CONT Perhaps her greatest achievement other than her family was the beautification project she took after retiring from the ranch when she and a life-long neighbor and friend, Mrs. Sophronia Petersen spent ten years beautifying the Lym
2 CONC an Cemetery. This beautiful place is now a living tribute to her work.
2 CONT Carrie Walker lived the thing that she would teach, an industrious life of love and service for her family, her neighbors, friends and community.
2 CONT Surviving are her husband Charles Walker of Lyman; three sons, Kemsley and Ren of Green River and Alfred of Ogden, Utah; five daughters, Mrs. James (Eileen) Pearce, Baldwin Park, Calif.; Mrs. M. F. (Amy) Seidenburg, Ventura, Calif
2 CONC .; Mrs. Leland (Thora) Bluemel, Lyman; Mrs. Lourin (Belva) Lowell, Green River, and Mrs. Glen (Fay) Wadsworth, Lonetree; 34 grandchildren, 72 great grandchildren and one great-great grandchild; three brothers and one sister.
2 CONT All the children were here for the funeral and many of the grandchildren. Mr. and Mrs. Reuben Kemsley of New Hollywood, Calif., and Mr. and Mrs. Clave Walker of Henderson, Nevada, were also here.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Transcribed from newspaper clipping from a family scrapbook)
