1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Charles H. Walker Passed Away Wednesday March 25th
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Henry Walker, 84, Lyman, Wyo., passed away Wednesday, March 25th in a Rock Springs rest home.
2 CONT He was born March 14, 1886, in Huntsville, Utah, the son of John E. and Maryett Colvin Walker. He married Carrie Kemsley on September 5, 1906 in Huntsville, Utah and later the marriage was solemnized in the Salt Lake Temple. Mrs. 
2 CONC Walker died September 11, 1968.
2 CONT He is survived by three sons and five daughters: Kemsley and Ren C. Walker and Mrs. Lourin (Belva) Lowell all of Green River, Wyoming; Alfred K. Walker, Ogden, Mrs. James (Eileen) Pearce, Baldwin Park, Calif., Mrs. M. F. (Amy) Sei
2 CONC denburg, Ventura, Calif., Mrs. Leland (Thora) Bluemel, Lyman, and Mrs. Glen (Fay) Wadsworth of Lonetree, Wyoming. Thirty-four grandchildren and 72 great grandchildren; one brother, Clave of Henderson, Nevada and one sister, Mrs. G
2 CONC eneva Moyes, Twin Falls, Idaho.
2 CONT Funeral services will be conducted Saturday, March 29 at 2:00 p. m. in the Lyman L. D. S. Chapel. Friends may call at the Gilbert Bills Funeral Home on Friday from 6:00 to 9:00 p. m. and at the chapel in Lyman on Saturday from 11:
2 CONC 00 a. m. until time of services. Burial will be in the Lyman cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT #2 clipping
2 CONT The family prayer was given by Clave Walker; opening prayer, Robert Bluemel; song, “In the Garden”, by Nola and Peggy Bluemel; obituary, J. Lowell Walker; talk, Clinton Bradshaw; song, “somewhere, My Love”, by Nola and Peggy Bluem
2 CONC el; talk, Edwin Davidson; song, “Oh, My Father” by Lyman Choir; closing prayer, Joe Fackrell. Pallbearers were Charles Pearce, Lex Wadsworth, Lawrence Lowell, Miles Walker, Reynold Bluemel, and Ted Walker, all of whom are grandson
2 CONC s.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Transcribed from a newspaper clippings from a family scrapbook)
