1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Death Claims Young Mother
2 CONT ------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Death came very unexpectedly and suddenly Monday to Mrs. Ava Maryett Walker Bluemel at her home just east of Lyman. Mrs. Bluemel had returned Friday from Ogden, where she had previously undergone an operation for appendicitis, whi
2 CONC ch operation she had recovered from very satisfactorily.
2 CONT Shortly before noon Mrs. Bluemel had stepped on to her porch and was watching her three small children playing in the yard. She swooned and fell. Her husband immediately rushed to her side. She complained of a severe pain over her
2 CONC  heart. Dr. Bosshardt was summoned at once, but was unable to prolong the life of the young mother. Cause of death was given as anginapectros of the heart.
2 CONT Mrs. Ava Walker Bluemel was born October 10, 1910 at Eden, Weber County, Utah. The daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Charles Walker. While still a small girl she moved with her parents to Lyman. She attended the Lyman public schools and gr
2 CONC aduated from the Lyman Junior High School. On October the 20, 1928 she was married to Leland Bluemel. She was the mother of three small children, two girls and a boy.
2 CONT News of her death came as a very severe shock to residents of the valley.
2 CONT The deceased is survived by her husband and children, her parents, and the following brothers and sisters; Leo C. Walker of Green River, Mrs. James Pearce, Mrs. Orsen Fackrell, Kemsley Walker, Thora Walker, Belva Walker, Fay Walke
2 CONC r, Ren Walker and Alfred Walker, all of Lyman.
2 CONT Funeral services were held today at 2 p. m. in the L. D. S. chapel at Lyman. The Lyman choir sang two beautiful hymns. Speakers were: Pres. H. Melvin Rollins, Pres. John C. Walker and Bishop Willis J. Lyman. A duet was sang by Mrs
2 CONC . Verna Bradshaw and Ervin Larsen. Another duet, “I Love You Truly” was sung by Mrs. Alvin Stewart and Mrs. H. Melvin Rollins. Burial was in the Lyman cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT (transcribed from a newspaper clipping in the family history book of Carrie Kemsley Walker)
