1 NAME Thomas /Wood/
2 GIVN Thomas
2 _MARNM Wood
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 ADDR
3 CITY Cromwell
3 STAE Otago
3 CTRY New Zealand
1 BIRT
2 DATE 10 FEB 1844
2 ADDR
3 CITY Brighton
3 STAE England
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 DEAT
2 DATE 19 OCT 1906
2 ADDR
3 CITY Cromwell
3 STAE Otago
3 CTRY New Zealand
1 OCCU Farmer 
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Thomas was born in 1844 in Brighton, Sussex, England. Thomas lived with his family in Maresfield, Sussex, before moving to London when he was about 17. he immigrated to New Zealand about 1864 atd the age of 20. He 
2 CONC soon travelled to Dunstan for the goldfields and spent his life based in and around lowburn.In 1867 he married Mary Ann Roberts in St Paul's Church Dunedin. They lived in Lowburn ferry nad had two children Fanny and Robert. Mary A
2 CONC nn died 1871 soon after the birth of their second child Robert. In late 1874 Thomas travelled to Dunedin with the view to remarry, and after six weeks he remarried Mary Fenwick Wilson. thomas and Mary had 10 children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is with great regret we have to announce the death of Thomas Wood, of Lowburn, aged 63 years, another of the fast diminishing band of early pioneers who opened up Central Otago. Deceased had been ailing for some time past, but 
2 CONC it was not known until the last few weeks that he was suffering from that dread disease cancer, and he passed away on Friday morning at his home, surrounded by hia sorrowing family. He was esteemed by one and all for his upright c
2 CONC haracter, was a great worker all his life, and was exceptionally fond of farming. He was born in Brighton, England, in 1813, and served his apprenticeship as a carpenter in London. He landed in Dunedin in 1864, and almost immediat
2 CONC ely came to this district, and has resided here ever since. As is usual with the early pioneers he had followed various occupations as ferryman, miner, carpenter and farmer. He was married twice, and leaves a widow, five sons, fou
2 CONC r daughters, and twelve grandchild run to mourn his loss.
2 CONT
2 CONT The funeral on Sunday, despite a terrific dust storm, was one of the largest ever seen in the district, numbers from all around the country attending to pay their last tribute of respect to the deceased. As he was one of the oldes
2 CONC t members of the Oddfellows (since the year 1871) a very large number of these attended. The service of the Lodge was read by Mr C, Ray at the grave, and tbe Rev. A. Reader also officiated at the grave in an impressive eorvke.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas appeared to be a resonable successful gold miner and farmer. His estate left when he died as estimated to be 2,245 pounds.  
