1 NAME Philadelphia /Newnham/
2 GIVN Philadelphia
2 SURN Newnham
2 _MARNM Vinall
2 NICK Widow Allchorne
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 21 APR 1768
2 ADDR
3 CITY Isfield
3 STAE England
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 BAPM
2 DATE 26 MAR 1769
2 ADDR
3 STAE Sussex
3 CTRY England
1 BURI
2 DATE 4 MAR 1853
2 ADDR
3 CITY St Mary
3 STAE Sussex
3 CTRY England
1 DEAT
2 DATE 4 MAR 1853
2 ADDR
3 CITY Buxted
3 STAE England
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Newnham-158 Dates (1769 - 1853)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Philadelphia Newnham''' was born in 1769 in Isfield, Sussex, the second of at least six children of Andrew Newnham and Elizabeth Pentecost [1]. Andrew was from Isfield and Elizabeth from Fletching, and they were married in Flet
2 CONC ching on 3-Dec-1761 [2].
2 CONT
2 CONT 1769: Philadelphia was baptised on 26-Mar-1769 at St Margarets in Isfield [3]. All 5 of Philadelphia's siblings ( Charlotta 1865 [4], Jane 1771 [5], George 1777 [6], Andrew 1780 [7], William 1783 [8] ) were born in Isfield, indica
2 CONC ting that Philadelphia was likely living in Isfield until she was at least 14 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1787: At the age of 18, Philadelphia married 31 year old Thomas Allchorne (born in Buxted, bap. 15-Aug-1756) on 11-Oct-1787 in Isfield, though the marriage process appears to have begun in Buxted, indicating that Philadephia might
2 CONC  have been living in Buxted [9] [10] [11].
2 CONT
2 CONT 1788-1797: Philadelphia and Thomas then had three children, all baptised in Buxted: Thomas 13-Apr-1788 [12], George 5-Jan-1794 [13], and John 16-Apr-1797 [14]. Thomas remained in Buxted, married Jane Killick (21-Nov-1820)[15] and 
2 CONC eventually died in Buxted on 30-Jun-1866 [16]. George had a more interesting life. In 1816, at the age of 22, George was in the Sussex Assizes charged with Larceny, but was acquitted [17]. In 1831 he was charged with receiving sto
2 CONC len good and convicted on 10-Dec-1831 [18]. He was sentenced to transportation for 7 years, and sent to Van Diemens Land, where he spent the rest of his life. He ended up reconnecting with his younger half-brother Jonathan Harris 
2 CONC (another convict) - and was a witness to Jonathan's third wedding in Tasmania in 1853. He died on 26-Jan-1867 in Tasmania at the age of 74 [19]. John, like his brother Thomas, remained in Buxted eventually died and was buried on 2
2 CONC 8-Oct-1832 [20].
2 CONT
2 CONT 1797: Thomas Allcorn, at the age of 41, died on 1-Oct-1797 in Buxted [21], leaving Philadelphia a widow.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1800: After being widowed Philadelphia may have returned to her home village of Isfield (even if only temporarily), given that in 1800 she appears to have commenced preparations for a marriage to John Harris in nearby Ringmer. The
2 CONC  parish register entry only contains names for Philadelphia and John, but no dates for Banns [22] and there is no associated Bishops transcript entry. No marriage record can be found in other nearby parished, and for her subsequen
2 CONC t marriage in 1802 she gave her name as Philly Alcorn, not Philly Harris. This all indicates that the marriage was not actually finalised. John Harris is likely to have been baptised in Ringmer on 21-Jan-1773 [23] There are possib
2 CONC le numerous death records for people named "John Harris" that can be located, but nothing definite. The only death record in the right time period is a John Harris, died in Brighton four days after her next marriage - so unlikely.
2 CONC  This all may indicate that John Harris was still alive when Philadelphia remarried - strengthening the conclusion that the marriage was not finalised.
2 CONT
2 CONT Irrespective of whether the marriage was finalised, Philadelphia and John's only child, Jonathan Harris, was born in 1800 and baptised in Mayfield [24] on 12-Oct-1800 [25]. His parents are listed as John Harris and Philadelphia Ha
2 CONC rris (and interestingly, he is not listed as illegitimate, presumably because his parents indicated they were married even though they nay not have been). Johnathan may have remained in Mayfield (or at least nearby Buxted) as his 
2 CONC marriage 21 years later occurred in Mayfield, and indicated him as being "of this parish". Jonathan eventually did follow his mother into a life of crime and was transported to Australia, but lived a long and fascinating life: thr
2 CONC ee wives, seventeen children, and dying on 4-Oct-1891 at the age of 91.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1802: On the 20-Apr-1802 Philly Alcorn [26] married Alexander Vinall in Ringmer [27][28] though they appear to have still lived in Buxted (given the birth location of their children). Alexander was baptised in Fletching (about 5 m
2 CONC iles from Isfield) on 23-Jan-1780 [29]. No death record for Alexander has been found, but Philadelphia is listed as a widow in the 1851 census.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1803-1808: Philadelphia and Alexander may have had at least three children - Alexander 1803, Edward 1806, and Martin 1808 - though definitive records for these children are unclear. A tree on the Weald site [30] lists all three, b
2 CONC ut birth records for them have not been found and so their connection to Philadelphia and Alexander has not been confirmed. 1851 census records for both Alexander [31] and Martin [32] list them and their families.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1817: At the age of 48 Philadelphia was in court, in the July quarter session charged with Larceny, but was found not guilty [33].
2 CONT
2 CONT 1818: A year later Philadelphia was again in court, in the Lent Assizes, charged with 'Uttering bad money'. Again, she was found not guilty [34]. Interestingly, this time her name is listed as Philidelphia Allcorn (rather than Vin
2 CONC all) - possibly indicating some issue with her last husband (maybe due to the multiple charges?), though later she was again using the name Vinall.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1825: Philadelphia (again using the name Vinall) was again in the Lent Assizes, this time charged with receiving stolen goods [35]. She was found guilty and convicted to 1 year imprisonment. In the same court sessions, Philadelphi
2 CONC a's son Jonathan Harris was also found guilty of buglary and convicted to transportation for life to Australia. There is a note on Jonathan's record that "Philadelphia possibly let off for giving kings evidence in Jonothan's case"
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT 1841: The 1841 census has Philadelphia, now aged 72, living in Buxted [36] (with numerous other Vinall's living nearby).
2 CONT
2 CONT 1851: The 1851 census still has Philadelphia (83yo) living in Buxted [37].
2 CONT
2 CONT 1853: Philadelphia died in Buxted, and was buried on 4-Mar-1853 at the age of 83 [38].
2 CONT
2 CONT Additional research notes
2 CONT
2 CONT One additional piece of evidence that strongly supports the conclusion that the three Philadelphia's (the wife of Thomas Allchorne; the partner of John Harris; and the wife of Alexander Vinall) are the same person, is a note on th
2 CONC e 1843 Tasmanian convict details for her son Jonathan Harris [39]. This includes a statement: "Remarks: Father: Jonathan at native place; 7 [sic] brothers: Thomas, George, John, Martin, Edward; 1 sister: Mary at native place.". Th
2 CONC e first three brothers are Jonathan's older Allchorne half-siblings. The last two brothers are Jonathan's younger Vinall half-siblings (though interestingly Alexander is missing).
2 CONT
2 CONT Two additional items are worth noting. Firstly this seems to suggest that Jonathan's father (also Jonathan) is still alive - or at least was when he last received information (possibly when his first wife emigrated in 1831). And s
2 CONC econdly, there is mention of a (living) sister, Mary, who has yet to be identified.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000196295375828&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sources
2 CONT # See http://theweald.org/N10.asp?NId=5673 and http://www.combedown.org/tng/familygroup.php?familyID=F106&tree=miller
2 CONT # "England Marriages, 1538–1973", database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:N6KM-P5D : 10 December 2014), Andrew Newnham and Elizabeth Pentecost, 03 Dec 1761; citing Fletching, Sussex, England, reference, ind
2 CONC ex based upon data collected by the Genealogical Society of Utah, Salt Lake City; FHL microfilm 1,067,170.
2 CONT # See Sussex Family History Group, http://sfhg.frontis.co/bin/aps_detail.php?id=469620
2 CONT # See http://sfhg.frontis.co/bin/aps_detail.php?id=469592
2 CONT # See http://sfhg.frontis.co/bin/aps_detail.php?id=469642
2 CONT # See http://sfhg.frontis.co/bin/aps_detail.php?id=469702
2 CONT # See http://sfhg.frontis.co/bin/aps_detail.php?id=469727
2 CONT # See http://sfhg.frontis.co/bin/aps_detail.php?id=469747
2 CONT # It appears that the Philadelphia and Thomas commenced their Banns in Buxted as there is an initial record in the Buxted parish register with just one date: 16-Sep-1787, but no matching confirmation in the Bishops Transcripts. "E
2 CONC ngland, Sussex, Parish Registers, 1538-1910", database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:KFG4-63Q: 15 July 2017), Thomas Alcorn and Philadelphia Newnham, 1787.
2 CONT # There is then a record in the Isfield parish records for a marriage three weeks later on 11-Oct-1787.
2 CONT # A number of internet sites have Philadelphia marrying a differing Thomas Alchorne - one baptised in Isfield on 19-Aug-1764 to John and Priscilla Alchorne (see http://sfhg.frontis.co/bin/aps_detail.php?id=469585). This Thomas is 
2 CONC much closer in age (4 years older) to Philadelphia and is born in the same place as Philadelphia, but other records show him marrying someone else (see http://theweald.org/N10.asp?NId=10030880).
2 CONT # Philadelphia's married name had numerous different spelling. Thomas's baptism shows his name spelt Allchorne. On the marriage it is shown as Alcorn. Their childrens baptisms were Alchorne, Allchorne, and Alcorne. Later records f
2 CONC or Philadelphia used both Allcorn and Alchorn.
2 CONT # "England Births and Christenings, 1538-1975," database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:JS5P-Y5R: 30 December 2014, Thomas Alchorne, 13 Apr 1788); citing Buxted, Sussex, England, index based upon data colle
2 CONC cted by the Genealogical Society of Utah, Salt Lake City; FHL microfilm 1,067,365.
2 CONT # "England Births and Christenings, 1538-1975," database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:NYHQ-MLQ: 30 December 2014, George Allchorne, 05 Jan 1794); citing Buxted, Sussex, England, index based upon data coll
2 CONC ected by the Genealogical Society of Utah, Salt Lake City; FHL microfilm 1,067,365.
2 CONT # "England Births and Christenings, 1538-1975," database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:N2S3-CVB  : 30 December 2014, John Alcorne, 16 Apr 1797); citing Buxted, Sussex, England, index based upon data collec
2 CONC ted by the Genealogical Society of Utah, Salt Lake City; FHL microfilm 1,067,365.
2 CONT # "England Marriages, 1538–1973 ," database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:NVFQ-DJM : 10 December 2014), Thomas Allchorne and Jane Killick, 21 Nov 1820; citing Buxted,Sussex,England, reference , index based
2 CONC  upon data collected by the Genealogical Society of Utah, Salt Lake City; FHL microfilm 1,067,366.
2 CONT # "England, Sussex, Parish Registers, 1538-1910", database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:KFPW-8MX : 15 July 2017), Thomas Alchorne, 1866.
2 CONT # Ancestry.com. England & Wales, Criminal Registers, 1791-1892. Class: HO 27; Piece: 12; Page: 437. See http://search.ancestry.com.au/cgi-bin/sse.dll?indiv=1&db=CriminalRegisters&h=1055299
2 CONT # Ancestry.com. England & Wales, Criminal Registers, 1791-1892. Class: HO 27; Piece: 44; Page: 305. See http://search.ancestry.com.au/cgi-bin/sse.dll?indiv=1&db=CriminalRegisters&h=103150
2 CONT # Ancestry.com.Australia, Death Index, l787-l985.
2 CONT # "England, Sussex, Parish Registers, 1538-1910", database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:KFPW-HDT : 15 July 2017), John Alchorne, 1832.
2 CONT # Note that some sites, including Weald, appear to have an incorrect death date from 1831, which matches a death for a John Alcorn in Hellingly.
2 CONT # Sussex Family History Group, see http://sfhg.frontis.co/bin/aps_detail.php?id=5531151
2 CONT # FamilySearch.com, Parish registers and poor law records for Ringmer Items 12 - 18 General register (baptisms, marriages, burials), 1605-1723; Marriages and burials, 1678-1770; Baptisms, 1717-1778; Marriages and banns, 1754-1823;
2 CONC  Baptisms and burials, 1770-1812. See https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:S3HY-D14J-XY?i=636&cat=85294
2 CONT # See Sussex Family History Group, http://sfhg.frontis.co/bin/aps_detail.php?id=659822. This is the only relevant record that can be found.
2 CONT # The reason for the baptism occurring in Mayfield is unknown. It may have been because John and Philadelphia were avoiding Isfield and Buxted because of the uncertain marriage status, or it may have been because the couple was st
2 CONC ill living in Buxted, but in a village between Buxted and Mayfield - such as Hadlow Down, which spanned the two parishes.
2 CONT #  "England, Sussex, Parish Registers, 1538-1910," database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:QJD6-P7C8 :accessed 1 May 2016), Jonathan Harris, Christening, Mayfield, Mayfield, Sussex, England, 12 Oct 1800; ci
2 CONC ting 00480, West Sussex County Record Office, Chichester; FHL microfilm 1,067,249.
2 CONT # This 'Philly Alcorn' can be connected to Philadelphia Newnham in various ways. She ends up being involved in criminal trials with her son Johnathan Harris. And later census records give her location and year of birth as matching
2 CONC  that of Philadelphia.
2 CONT # "England, Sussex, Parish Registers, 1538-1910", database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:KFGT-7FF : 15 July 2017), Alexander Vinall and Philly Alchorn, 1802.
2 CONT # It is unclear why the marriage occurred in Ringmer, given that Alexander was from Fletching and Philadelphia was from Isfield. It may be related to the previous 'marriage' that was not finalised with John Harris.
2 CONT # Only a single relevant birth record for an Alexander Vinall can be located: "England Births and Christenings, 1538-1975," database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:NDZ3-7NL : 30 December 2014, Alexander Vin
2 CONC all, ); citing Fletching, Sussex, England, index based upon data collected by the Genealogical Society of Utah, Salt Lake City; FHL microfilm 1,067,170.
2 CONT # See http://theweald.org/N10.asp?NId=5673
2 CONT # "England and Wales Census, 1851"", database with images, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:SGJV-PTV : 29 July 2017), Alexander Vinall, Buxted, Sussex, England; citing Buxted, Sussex, England, p. 10, from "185
2 CONC 1 England, Scotland and Wales census," database and images, findmypast (http://www.findmypast.com : n.d.); citing PRO HO 107, The National Archives of the UK, Kew, Surrey.
2 CONT #  "England and Wales Census, 1851/' database with images, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:SGJV-PRR: 29 July 2017), Martin Vinall, Buxted, Sussex, England; citing Buxted, Sussex, England, p. 10, from "1851 En
2 CONC gland, Scotland and Wales census," database and images, findmypast (http://www.findmypast.com  : n.d.); citing PRO HO 107, The National Archives of the UK, Kew, Surrey.
2 CONT # Ancestry.com. England & Wales, Criminal Registers, 1791-1892. Class: HO 27; Piece: 14; Page: 232. See http://search.ancestry.com.au/cgi-bin/sse.dll?indiv=1&db=CriminalRegisters&h=1059852
2 CONT # Ancestry.com. England & Wales, Criminal Registers, 1791-1892. Class: HO 27; Piece: 16; Page: 212. See http://search.ancestry.com.au/cgi-bin/sse.dll?indiv=1&db=CriminalRegisters&h=1071033
2 CONT #  Ancestry.com. England & Wales, Criminal Registers, 1791-1892. Original data: Home Office: Criminal Registers, Middlesex and Home Office: Criminal Registers, England and Wales; Source Citation: Class: HO 27; Piece: 30; Page: 204
2 CONC . See http://search.ancestry.com.au/cgi-bin/sse.dll?indiv=1&db=CriminalRegisters&h=1096212
2 CONT # Ancestry.com. 1841 England Census. Original data: Census Returns of England and Wales, 1841. Kew, Surrey, England: The National Archives of the UK (TNA): Public Record Office (PRO), 1841. Data imaged from the National Archives, 
