1 NAME Ann /Lucas/
2 GIVN Ann
2 SURN Lucas
2 _MARNM Williams - Faro
2 NICK Ann Williams
2 NICK Ann Faro
1 SEX F
1 BURI
2 PLAC Dunedin, Dunedin City, Otago, New Zealand
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 2 MAR 1789
2 PLAC Norfolk Island
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Norfolk Island
1 DEAT
2 DATE 15 JUL 1864
2 ADDR
3 CITY Dunedin
3 STAE Otago
3 CTRY New Zealand
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ann Lucas  	Born 2nd March 1789. By James Donohoe
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the first European female to be born on Norfolk Island, and was recorded as Ann Gaskins, she was the eldest of thirteen children. Her birthplace would have been in the vicinity of the present town of Kingston, behind the c
2 CONC onvict era administrative area.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann was raised on Norfolk Island and lived there until she was 16 years of age. In March 1805 she moved to Sydney, New South Wales, with her parents and brothers and sisters on HMS “Investigator”, which earlier had been in the com
2 CONC mand of Matthew Flinders for his cartographic and scientific voyages.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann was recorded still illiterate at the age of eighteen
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 2nd December, 1807, Ann married Charles William's a fellow Norfolk Islander, at St. Philip's Church, Sydney, N.S.W. The Rev. Harry Fulton, who was also a convict on Norfolk Island celebrated the marriage. The witness to the
2 CONC  marriage was Arthur Devlin, the famed Irish rebel who later married Ann’s brother's sister-in-law, Priscilla Squire. Arthur and Priscilla settled in Kingston, Tasmania near former Marine, Thomas Lucas.
2 CONT
2 CONT She gave birth to four children in Sydney and another six were born in Launceston, VDL. She had moved to Launceston in 1817 with her mother and some of her brothers and sisters. In her own right she acquired a grant of land on the
2 CONC  southern bank of the River Tamar north of the settlement. It was adjacent to the grants made to some of her brothers and sisters.
2 CONT
2 CONT When her mother obtained her own grant she entered into an arrangement with Ann [probably because she was the eldest] as tenant-in-common, and on Olivia’s death in 1830, Olivia’s land passed to her. Ann sold the land in 1836 and i
2 CONC t appears she distributed the proceeds amongst the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1835 Ann’s husband disappeared in “mysterious circumstances” {believed to have been murdered on the Pyrmont Bridge in Sydney} and in 1837 she gave birth to an “illegitimate” child whose name was registered in 1838 as France
2 CONC s Faro, the father being a Thomas Faro, a baker who was also the licensee of the Half Moon Hotel in Tamar St., Launceston.
2 CONT
2 CONT The couple married in April 1840 and then travelled to Melbourne where they lived for a brief time before setting in the Portland Bay area where Thomas was employed by William Learmouth to run his Dairy.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the 1860’s Ann, now widowed, travelled to Dunedin, New Zealand with her youngest daughter Frances, her son-in-law Edward Manson and her grandchildren. In 1864 on 15th July, Ann Faro, formerly Williams, nee Gascoyne [also sp
2 CONC elt Gaskin and Gascoigne died in the Dunedin Hospital aged 75 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Ann Lucas and Charles Williams.
2 CONT
2 CONT A. Charles 	F. Sarah
2 CONT
2 CONT B. William 	G. Mary Ann
2 CONT
2 CONT C. George 	E. Nathaniel
2 CONT
2 CONT D. Ann 	F. Maria
2 CONT
2 CONT E. Olivia 	G. Thomas 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ann's 2nd marriage was to Thomas Faro with whom she had a relationship with before the death of his 1st wife.  Soon after their marriage the family moved to Melbourne, Ann in October 1840 and Thomas in December 1840.  After thomas
2 CONC  died in 1859 Ann moved to New Zealand in the early 1860's with her daughter Frances and son-in-law Edward Manson.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-1-290175591-1-515557/ann-williams-born-lucas-in-myheritage-family-trees?indId=externalindividual-a37f9bc43c2538985c0e6d1ffe8ba61a&mrid=c69c081148ff046d7a024d8b0e34bbfd [obje
2 CONC ct Object]] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jul 31 2019, 4:22:32 UTC''
