1 NAME William Nathaniel John /Lucas/
2 GIVN William Nathaniel John
2 SURN Lucas
2 _MARNM Lucas
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 7 JAN 1792
2 PLAC Norfolk Island
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Norfolk Island
1 DEAT
2 DATE JUL 1828
2 ADDR
3 CITY Parsley Bay, Sydney Harbour
3 STAE New South Wales
3 CTRY Australia
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} William Lucas Born 7th January, 1792. By James Donohoe.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Lucas was born on the 7th January, 1792, the son of Nathaniel Lucas and Olivia Gascoyne, at Sydney, Norfolk Island. William was officially recorded by the Administration as William Gaskins, along with his elder sisters. Hi
2 CONC s name reverted to William (Nathaniel) Lucas administratively on his transfer to Sydney in 1805.
2 CONT
2 CONT As no trace can be made of his actual transport to Sydney nor to other locations where his presence was noted, it seems that he travelled as a crew member on the ship. Evidence of an interest in ships exists and this activity is s
2 CONC een as contributing to his early death.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was illiterate. In 1824 William gave evidence to a judicial inquiry into an incident at New Norfolk, V.D.L., in which a number of bush rangers raided the homestead of the McCarthy family, whom William had known on Norfolk 
2 CONC Island and had been visiting at the time. An “X” marked his Statement.
2 CONT
2 CONT This is consistent too with his marriage registration. However he was a skilled tradesman.
2 CONT
2 CONT In particular, he was recorded as a boat builder in his early years. It appears that his father taught him the art of carpentry along with his brothers and included boat building in skills passed on.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 1st May, 1812, William married Sarah Squire, the daughter of James Squire, a brewer and businessman of Kissing Point and Elizabeth Mason, at St. Philip’s Church of England, Sydney.
2 CONT
2 CONT William entered business in 1812 as a Publican, operating the Trafalgar Hotel in Princes Street, now the site of the toll-gates on the Sydney Harbour Bridge. His first son George was born there. Probably his eldest daughter Sarah 
2 CONC was born there too.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 16th November, 1816, William went to Launceston seeking building contracts. He was followed by his brother Nathaniel, then brother John. In 1817, his mother and her remaining children joined them. The boys secured a contrac
2 CONC t including the Gaol there and a Chapel.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family took a particular interest in hop, barley and wheat farming and it appears that Olivia took the family to Launceston to develop a supply of wheat which would synchronize with the family windmill operations at Liverpool 
2 CONC still being run by Nathaniel. John also built a windmill at Liverpool called Brisbane Watermill which augmented production.
2 CONT
2 CONT William purchased 900 acres of land from Robert Campbell and secured a further grant of 100 acres fronting the South Esk River at it's junction with the Meander River, south of Launceston, where the family grew wheat and some barl
2 CONC ey for the Squire Brewery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Originally, the family shipped the grain to New South Wales on ships owned by their former neighbours in Sydney, John Palmer and Robert Campbell. Some family members returned to Sydney with their cargo from time to time.
2 CONT
2 CONT William’s father Nathaniel died in April 1818 causing the family to return to New South Wales in July, 1818 to pay their respects and to tidy his affairs. His father at the time was heavily in debt. A Mr. John Terry took over the 
2 CONC Administration of the estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT The loss of the Liverpool operation changed the perspective of the family business and vested in William a greater responsibility for the various family's financial affairs, a role for which he was poorly equipped. The land at Lau
2 CONC nceston was later alienated to Youl estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, his mother, sought and obtained a further grant of 100 acres in her own right. This allowed the wheat growing side of the business to continue.
2 CONT
2 CONT William and Sarah had another three children while living in Launceston, William (1819), James Bradford (1820), and Martha (1822).
2 CONT
2 CONT In due course, their building business and wheat farming activities failed. William then returned to Sydney aboard the cutter “Governor Arthur” in July 1826. Olivia followed about a week later, alone.
2 CONT
2 CONT William and John recommenced business in Liverpool as builders and won a contract to build the Court House there in early 1827. Not long after commencing construction their business failed again.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, Sarah and her four surviving children returned to Liverpool, travelling to Sydney aboard the “Hetty”. When the business failed William got a job as a storekeeper. In September 1827, William and Sarah had another son, Na
2 CONC thaniel.
2 CONT
2 CONT William’s business failures persisted throughout the first half of 1828. In the Sydney Gazette of the 30th May 1828, Richard Guise published a notice which mentioned that William had surreptitiously obtained notes from him and cau
2 CONC tioned the public against negotiating them. He repeated the notice in the edition of the 4th June.
2 CONT
2 CONT In July 1828 William was aboard a ship on Sydney Harbour. He was either knocked out or fell overboard while the ship passed Parsley Bay. His body was not recovered. There were suspicious circumstances surrounding his death. No rec
2 CONC ords of an inquest have been found.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, there is some speculation that whist inebriated at a popular Sydney Hotel (possibly the Orient), he was "Shanghaied" by sailors. It is reported that upon coming to, the ship was on its way out of the harbour. When William
2 CONC  discovered his siuation, he struggled violently with the ships master, was knocked out and duly deposited over the side of the ship.
2 CONT
2 CONT William’s poor business sense and early death rendered his family destitute. By the end of 1828 his son George had been left to his own resources (he stayed with his grandmother Olivia Lucas)., whilst his daughter Martha, and anot
2 CONC her son James were placed in the Orphanage at Cabramatta. Sarah was to survive only with the help of an assigned convict Charles Turtle.
2 CONT
2 CONT William’s brother John was declared insolvent in September, 1828 and pursued by the Audit Office of the British Government until as late as 1844 for the advance for the Liverpool Court House construction. The Sheriff sold up Natha
2 CONC niel’s land grant at Minto on the 24th March, 1829, forcing Olivia to return to Launceston permanently.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of William Lucas and Sarah Squire.
2 CONT
2 CONT A. George 	E. Martha
2 CONT
2 CONT B. Sarah 	F. John
2 CONT
2 CONT C. William 	G. Nathaniel Bradford
2 CONT
2 CONT D. James 	 
