2 ADDR
3 CITY Longford
3 STAE Tasmania
3 CTRY Australia
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} George Lucas Born 8th May, 1800. By Richard Hodgetts, Tasmania.
2 CONT
2 CONT George Lucas was the ninth child of Nathaniel and Olivia Lucas. He was born on the 8th May, 1800 on Norfolk Island, and his baptism was registered at the Church of St. Philip, Church Hill, Sydney, New South Wales. On the 24th Febr
2 CONC uary, 1823 he married Elizabeth Hodgetts at the Church of St. John, Launceston, Van Diemen's Land. George and Elizabeth had known each other from childhood, having grown up together on Norfolk Island and later New South Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT The two families remained close, George's sister Olivia having married Elizabeth's brother, John. Two other Lucas children, William and Mary Ann Lucas married Sarah Squire and Samuel Cox, from other families connected to the Hodge
2 CONC tts. For the first years of their marriage George Lucas worked as a carpenter as had most of his brothers. He was granted 260 acres on the Longford to Cressy main road and here he and Elizabeth built their first home, a small weat
2 CONC her board house. Their first child, Elizabeth, was born here and a string of sons soon followed.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1831, land near the Lucas grant became vacant and following regulations, George submitted a formal application for land as follows: Sir, I request you will be pleased to submit to His Excellency the Lieutenant Governor, through
2 CONC  the proper channel, this my application for an additional grant of land without purchase. I am in possession of 260 acres by grant of which 75 are under cultivation. I posses 21 head of cattle, no sheep, 2 horses. I have erected 
2 CONC a weather boarded house of 3 rooms and a weather boarded kitchen. I employ one convict servant.
2 CONT
2 CONT List of George Lucas's effects.
2 CONT
2 CONT 17 milch cows with calves
2 CONT
2 CONT working bullocks
2 CONT
2 CONT mare and foal
2 CONT
2 CONT 450 bushels of wheat
2 CONT
2 CONT 200 bushels of oats
2 CONT
2 CONT Dwelling house and kitchen
2 CONT
2 CONT Two miles of three railed fencing
2 CONT
2 CONT 54 rods of paling fencing around house and garden
2 CONT
2 CONT 36 rods of paling fence around stock yard Cart, plough and harrows
2 CONT
2 CONT 35 0 0 post and rails for fencing 60 0 0 Cash on hand
2 CONT
2 CONT 	42 10 0 4
2 CONT
2 CONT 30 0 0 1
2 CONT
2 CONT 80 0 0
2 CONT
2 CONT 90 0 0
2 CONT
2 CONT 35 0 0
2 CONT
2 CONT 110 0 0
2 CONT
2 CONT 114 0 0
2 CONT
2 CONT 20 0 0
2 CONT
2 CONT 15 0 0
2 CONT
2 CONT 3000
2 CONT
2 CONT 100 0 0
2 CONT
2 CONT He also supplied the following testimonials.
2 CONT
2 CONT We the undersigned do hereby testify that Mr. George Lucas is of respectable character and we have with every reason to believe the statement in this letter to the Surveyor General of his stock and effects, the value of improvemen
2 CONC ts made on the land he holds and the quantity of fencing completed thereon, is true and correct.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Hortle and Wm. Brumby April 23rd, 1831
2 CONT
2 CONT I have the honour to certify for the information of his Excellency the Lieutenant Governor, that the applicant George Lucas is a married man of sober and industrious habits and altogether a most deserving character.
2 CONT
2 CONT Malcolm Smith. Police Magistrate
2 CONT
2 CONT A report from the Lands Board on his application:
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. George Lucas who attended the Board is a native of Norfolk Island, received from Governor Macquarie a grant of 260 acres situated at Norfolk Plains of which 75 acres are under tillage, the whole of which will be fenced in the 
2 CONC course of ten days. He has erected a weather boarded house and made other improvements as stated in his letter of application. Is married and has four children. The township (Longford) adjoins his present land and he believes ther
2 CONC e is some vacant land near him.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Board assenting to the excellent character of the applicant and in consideration of the improvements which he has effected upon his grant, and his being a native of these colonies, recommends that the land be granted to him on
2 CONC  the usual conditions of such location.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lands Board Office, 30th May, 1831.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth and George Lucas received their grant of further land and immediately began to excavate the site for a new brick house, starting with a deep underground cellar. Elizabeth and George worked very hard throughout the follow
2 CONC ing years, completing their new house, establishing a first class farm and all the while, adding to their family.
2 CONT
2 CONT The census of December 31st, 1842, showed that they were proprietors of a fine brick house, had 8 children living with them and were employing 4 ticket-of-leave men. The name of the house and the property was and is still known as
2 CONC  "Kelton". "Kelton" was built of locally made bricks (the pit can still be seen), hand-made nails, cedar doors and window frames, banisters and skirting's. It had a Georgian front with a cedar door and fan light, a long brick kitc
2 CONC hen with a bread oven and colonial fire place.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was an outside door leading down to the extensive cellar which had wide shelves around and which was used as a dairy. Two deep wells and windmills supplied the homestead with water.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 2nd July, 1845, "Kelton" was robbed by the bush ranger, Priest. He had waited until the men had left the house and then managed to hide himself in the cellar. When night came he pushed open a trapdoor which led into the sto
2 CONC rehouse. He replenished his food supply and without notice he slipped away to continue his life of crime. In fact it was said of Priest that there were no acts of violence attributed to him and that he only begged at farms. He had
2 CONC  been at large for three years and three months and not long after robbing "Kelton", he was journeying over rough country in the Longford district when his gun went off, its contents lodging in one of his feet. He crawled for thre
2 CONC e miles into Longford and surrended to the police. He was sentenced to death, but, as the result of petitions, this was commuted to transportation for life, ten years to be past on Norfolk Island.
2 CONT
2 CONT George and Elizabeth Lucas at this time had just completed their large family of 11 children, 10 still alive.
2 CONT
2 CONT Social life was restricted to visiting close family and neighbours, with so few people living in the district. Many of George and Elizabeth's children married their first cousins because of this social restriction. George and Eliz
2 CONC abeth Lucas were respected as great pioneers of the Longford district and they survived the terrors of bush rangers, droughts, depression and sickness.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is said that a sister of George Lucas was murdered by the Aborigines and her twin babies taken by them, being reared unsuccessfully on dissolved brown sugar. This sister was most likely Sarah Lucas who had married James Hortle
2 CONT
2 CONT George and Elizabeth Lucas remained at "Kelton for the rest of their days where George died on the 23rd of July, 1868, and Elizabeth died on the 23rd November, 1887. The property passed to their son Edward who continued to farm it
2 CONC  successfully until shortly before his death. "Kelton" was sold to the Walker family in 1901 and it remains in their possession to this day.
2 CONT
2 CONT The original weather board house fell down in the 1920's. The brick Georgian house has been added to, over the years and its original front has been covered over, however, the house still retains a character that is easily identif
2 CONC iable with the pioneering family that built and lived in it.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sale of "Kelton" saw the last of the Lucas family in Longford, however, "Kelton" remains as a tribute to a hard working and enterprising pioneer family. George and Elizabeth Lucas arrived in Longford when it contained a few sm
2 CONC all cottages and an inn, surrounded by a handful of settlers trying to scratch out a living on their small grants of land.
2 CONT
2 CONT When George and Elizabeth died, Longford had grown to be one of the main centres for agriculture in the north of the State. The Lucas family was one of many that helped Longford develop from a hostile environment to be one of civi
2 CONC lized development and prosperity.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of George and Elizabeth Lucas.
2 CONT
2 CONT A. Elizabeth 	G. Charles
2 CONT
2 CONT B. George 	H. Adelaide
2 CONT
2 CONT C. Thomas 	I. Edward
2 CONT
2 CONT D. John 	J. Emily
2 CONT
2 CONT E. William 	K. Albert George
2 CONT
2 CONT F. Daniel 	 
