1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Stanley Herbert Hayes, a well known resident of Albury, died yesterday. aged 62 years. ' He ha been in ailing health for the /past two years. His wife died in October last year, Mr. Hayes is survived by his sons Ja
2 CONC mes Hayes, who is manager of the Lockhart branch of Dalgety and Co -Ltd., and Mr Charles Hayes, Melbourne and by his daughter, Mrs. Walter Cameron Melbourne. His" only surviving brother is Mr. Fred Hayes, Dimboola Victoria, and si
2 CONC ster is lMrs W.M. Coverdale.-New castle. Mr. Stanley Hayes was a native of Albury. In his early life he joined the staff of the Commercial Banking Company of Sydney, and was in the bank's service in Queensland. His father, the lat
2 CONC e James Hayes, M.L.C., was among the pioneer residents of Albury, and was well known in politcal circles a generation ago. When the old Hume eleclorate returned two members, Mr Hayes and the-late Sir William I»yine were its repres
2 CONC entatives in the State Parliament for several years. The late Mr. Stanley Hayes, when he retired from the service of the Commercial Bank joined with his brother Fred the flour-milling business for several years they conducted the 
2 CONC mill in Townsend. street. The explosion of a steam boiler wrecked the mill, and the industry was discontinued In later years Mr. Hayes conducted several agencies. A likeable man, Mr. Hayes had many friends in Albury and district. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1934 'ABOUT PEOPLE', Border Morning Mail (Albury, NSW : 1934 - 1935; 1938 - 1954), 6 December, p. 4. , viewed 26 July 2025, http://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article271718860
2 CONT
