2 CONT '''Lithgow Mercury (NSW : 1898 - 1954)   Mon 20 Jul 1942  Page 2  DEATHS IN LITHGOW'''
2 CONT MR. R. KIRK
2 CONT Mr. Robert Willard Thomas Hemstead Kirk, one of our oldest residents, died in Lithgow hospital early on Friday afternoon at the age of 82 years. Born at Richmond, the deceased came io Lithgow over 67 years ago, residing here ever 
2 CONC since, and followed farming and timber-getting during that period.. One of the pioneers of this district, the late Mr. Kirk was most active for his years, and took an interest, as he himself used to put it, "in anything that was g
2 CONC oing," until he entered hospital three weeks ago.
2 CONT A popualr figure throughout the town and its environs the deceased had always been of a happy nature and was quick to make friends.
2 CONT Predeceased some 20 years by his wife, the late Mr. Kirk is survived by one son, Mr. Robert Kirk, of South Bowenfels, and one daughter,Mrs. O. Sharwood, of 41 Stephenson street, Lithgow.
2 CONT he funeral moved from the parlors of Messrs Matthews and Lemon this morning after service conducted by the Rev. E. Fletcher, who also officiated at the graveside. There were numerous floral tributes, two of which were of the perma
2 CONC nent type from the family. The funeral was particularly well-attended by residents of South Bowenfels. Interment was made in the Church of England portion of the cemetery, the remains being laid to rest beside those of his late wi
2 CONC fe. Pall-bearers were Messrs. J. Brown, O. Sharwood, J. Goodwin and B. Baker.
