2 CONT '''Lithgow Mercury (NSW : 1898 - 1954)  Wed 7 Dec 1938  Page 1  HARTLEY VALE RESIDENT'''
2 CONT HARTLEY VALE
2 CONT RESIDENT
2 CONT Dies After Long Illness Mrs. Louise Almira Matilda Gurney, who died in Lewisham hospital,Sydney, on Monday afternoon, at the age of 67, had been a resident of Hartley Vale for the past 45 years, and was exceptionally popular and w
2 CONC ell respected. She is survived by Messrs. Charles and Eric Gurney, of Hartley Vale, and Mrs. H. Oades, of Ashfleld, sons and daughter, respectively. The late Mrs. Gurney was born in
2 CONT Sydney, but spent practically all her life in the district. She was a daughter of the late Mr. and Mrs. R. Kirk, of Duckmaloi, and lived there before her marriage. Her husband, Mr. Albert Gurney, died in tragic circumstances in a 
2 CONC burning accident about three years ago. Mrs. Gurney's health has not been satisfactory for some time, and she entered the hospital on Thursday last. The funeral took place yesterday afternoon, interment being made in the Church of
2 CONC  England portion of Mt. York cemetery. The Rev. W. P.F. Dorph, of Mt. Victoria, conducted a short service at Messrs, Matthews and Lemon's parlors, Lithgow, and also at the graveside, where the remains were interred beside those of
2 CONC  her husband.
2 CONT There was a large gathering of mourners, who were representative of practically every interest in the Hartley district. Floral tributes were numerous. The pall-bearers were Messrs. C. Gurney, E. Gurney (sons),
2 CONT H. Kirk (brother) and H. Oades (son-in-law).
2 CONT The Rev. Mr. Dorph spoke in the highest terms of Mrs. Gurney as a mother and neighbor. He said she was a very fine type of woman.
