1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *Samuel served in the American Revolutionary War for Connecticut - DAR Ancestor # A114523 - https://services.dar.org/public/dar_research/search_adb/default.cfm
2 CONT Service Source:  MULLEN, CT TOWN MEETING RECS, VOL 2, PP 31, 34, 36
2 CONT Service Description:  1) SURVEYOR OF HIGHWAYS, 1780, 1781, 1782
2 CONT —-
2 CONT Biographical Profile
2 CONT Full Name: Samuel Thompson (often recorded as Thompson Jr. in early town records)
2 CONT Birth Date: August 1, 1737 (New Haven Colony, British America)
2 CONT Death Date: February 1817 (East Haven, New Haven County, Connecticut, USA)
2 CONT Parents: Samuel Thompson Sr. and Hannah Hemingway
2 CONT Spouse: Desire Holt (Married September 12, 1759, in East Haven)
2 CONT -Samuel and Desire had four primary children who survived into adulthood and are recognized in early East Haven town vital structures:
2 CONT 1. Jared Thompson
2 CONT Born: June 24, 1762 (East Haven, CT)Died: February 25, 1813 (Ulysses, Tompkins County, NY)-Marriages: Married twice: first to Lydia Blakeslee (1786) and second to Grace Hunt (1797).
2 CONT 2. Joel Thompson
2 CONT Born: Estimated circa 1764–1766 (East Haven, CT)Details: Lived in New Haven County during the post-Revolutionary War reconstruction period.
2 CONT 3. Samuel Thompson (III)
2 CONT Born: September 1, 1773 (East Haven, CT) Details: Carried on the family name in New Haven County, often cited in local agricultural land trades.
2 CONT 4. Desire Thompson
2 CONT Born: Estimated circa 1775 (East Haven, CT)-Marriage: Married a Mr. Smith.
2 CONT Primary Residence: East Haven / New Haven, Connecticut
2 CONT Patriot Service: Private / Soldier in the Connecticut Militia / Continental Line during the American Revolution.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Birth and Parentage Evidence (Establishing Identity)
2 CONT Town Vital Records: Look for the Vital Records of New Haven, 1649–1850. This contains his August 1, 1737 birth entry under his parents, Samuel and Hannah.Local Genealogies: Reference The East Haven Register by Stephen Dodd (publis
2 CONC hed 1824). This historical text lists the early Thompson family structures, marriages, and birth dates in East Haven.
2 CONT The Will of Samuel Thompson Sr.: If available, probate records from the New Haven District Court showing Samuel Sr. naming his son Samuel in his estate distribution will solidify the father-son link.
2 CONT 2. Revolutionary War Service Proof (Establishing Patriot Status)Because your target dates match DAR Ancestor #A114523, his service is already an established lineage branch. To cite it, use:
2 CONT The Rolls of Connecticut Men in the French and Indian War and Revolutionary War: Look up the compiled service rosters by the Connecticut Historical Society. He is recorded as serving in the 2nd Connecticut Regiment (1775) under Ge
2 CONC neral Joseph Spencer.
2 CONT Muster and Pay Rolls: National Archives (NARA) Microfilm Publication M246 (Revolutionary War Rolls). You can find digitized copies of these specific muster slips on military databases like Fold3 by Ancestry.
2 CONT 3. Marriage, Death, and Lineage Continuation (Establishing the Link to You)Marriage Record: The September 12, 1759 marriage to Desire Holt is recorded in Connecticut Church Record Abstracts (First Congregational Church of East Hav
2 CONC en).U.S. Federal Census Records (1790, 1800, 1810): Locate "Samuel Thompson" in New Haven County, Connecticut, to prove residency and household consistency through the post-war era.
2 CONT Probate and Land Records (1817): Look up the New Haven Probate District files for February–March 1817. His estate administration or will documents the distribution of his property to his surviving children, which is the vital "bri
2 CONC dge" needed to link him to the next generation in your tree.
2 CONT Cemetery/Burial Records: Search local East Haven transcriptions or historical headstone surveys, as many early Patriots are buried in the Old Graveyard (Green Cemetery) in East Haven.
