1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Birth Date: 
2 CONT March 1768 (Note: While some public family trees estimate a late 1773 date based on unverified baptism gaps, the official documented genealogical standard published in early Connecticut family volumes fixes his birth specifically 
2 CONC to March 1768).
2 CONT Birthplace: East Haven, New Haven County, Connecticut.
2 CONT Death Details: He passed away in New Haven County, Connecticut. -Unlike his brother Jared, who migrated to the Finger Lakes region of New York, Samuel III remained local to the family's ancestral lands.
2 CONT Spouse: Sarah Holt
2 CONT He and Sarah had a large household, including:
2 CONT 1. Daniel Thompson: Born in May 1787; he later married Susan Ann Bradley in January 1805 and migrated to Ulysses, Tompkins County, New York.
2 CONT 2. Asenath Thompson: Born March 20, 1801, in East Haven. She later married Ezra Hemenway and raised five children.
2 CONT 3. Kenath Thompson: Born March 20, 1801 (twin to Asenath), recorded in early East Haven birth books.
2 CONT 4. Samuel Thompson IV: Born circa 1803; carried the multi-generational family name forward in Connecticut land trades.
2 CONT
2 CONT Occupation & Land:
2 CONT He operated as a yeoman farmer and landowner. In early 19th-century New Haven County deed indexes, he is recorded alongside his father in local land conveyances, retaining property near the East Haven and Fair Haven borders.
2 CONT An Overlooked Generation: Because Samuel III was born just before the Revolutionary War, he was too young to fight in the Revolution, and too old to serve in the War of 1812. Consequently, he does not have military bounty land war
2 CONC rants or federal pension files, making his paper trail dependent almost entirely on local Connecticut property deeds and town vital record books.
