2 CONT ==================
2 CONT BANNER>, <Richard>. Event Court records 20 Jul 1877 Place: Victoria Source: Supreme Court of Victoria: Equity case files. Pt 1 Calendar of 'Miscellaneous' Equity case files 1852 -1886. Public Record Office Victoria: VPRS 12025/P1.
2 CONC  Author/compiler: Ackerly, Ada. p./no. Unit 16 case 684 Background information: Click here Comment: Former boatman of Sandridge, the residue of his estate divided between his son Walter & grandaughter Edith, daughter of Richard Ge
2 CONC orge Banner who is a broom manufacturer of Carlton; Category: Testator Port Melb.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Argus (Melbourne, Vic. Saturday 9 December 1871
2 CONT
2 CONT The new clipper cutter EDITH KATE, the most convenient boat in the bay, leaves the old Sandridge Pier every Sunday, at half past 2, for a whole AFTERNOON'S SAIL round the bay, calling at the Railway and St Kilda Piers for passenge
2 CONC rs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fare for the afternoon, Is. each.
2 CONT
2 CONT P.S.-Excursions to Western Port, Phillip Island, and other places of Interest attended to, at low rates, RICHARD BANNER.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Argus (Melbourne, Vic.  Saturday 23 March 1872
2 CONT
2 CONT SHIPPING INTELLIGENCE.
2 CONT
2 CONT HOBSON'S BAY.
2 CONT
2 CONT  ENTERED OUTWARDS.-MARCH 21.
2 CONT
2 CONT St Kilda, 189 tons, J. H. GILL, for Port Natal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edith and Kate, 10 tons, Richard Banner, for King's Island.
2 CONT
2 CONT  CLEARED OUT.-MARCH 22.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sea Nymph, 240 tons, W. H Ford, for Newcastle, in ballast James Paterson agent <
2 CONT
2 CONT Edith Kate, 10 tons, Richard Banner, for King's Island, In ballast. Geo Hurry, agent. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Argus (Melbourne, Vic. Thursday 27 August 1874
2 CONT
2 CONT  SATURDAY, AUGUST 29.
2 CONT
2 CONT SANDRIDGE.
2 CONT
2 CONT On tho Old Town Pier, at Three O'Clock.
2 CONT
2 CONT To Yachtsmen, Boatmen, and Others.
2 CONT
2 CONT A. E. AITKEN has been favoured with Instructions
2 CONT
2 CONT from the executors In the estate of the late   Mr. Richard Banner to SUBMIT to PUBLIC COMPETITION the well-known
2 CONT
2 CONT Cutter EDITH KATE,
2 CONT
2 CONT Coppered and copper-fastened, with sails, anchor and chains, dingy, and everything complete.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Edith Kate is reported to be the fastest, safest, and most comfortable yacht in Hobson's Bay, and is at present In first-class order, having been built about three years ago under the personal supervision of
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Banner.
2 CONT
2 CONT She can be inspected by intending purchasers on applying to Mr. Alf. Holland, Railway Pier, Sandridge.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Argus (Melbourne, Vic. Saturday 5 September 1874
2 CONT
2 CONT IN the SUPREME COURT of the COLONY of VICTORIA: 
2 CONT
2 CONT In its Probate Jurisdiction.-In the Will of RICHARD BANNER, late of Sandridge, in the Colony of Victoria, Boatman, Deceased-All persons having any CLAIMS against this estate are required to SEND the same to the undersigned, on or 
2 CONC before the tenth day of October next, after which day I, the undersigned, as executor of the said will, will proceed to distribute the said estate
2 CONT
2 CONT Dated this fourth day of September, 1874
2 CONT
2 CONT PHILIP LEIGH, 5 Leigh terrace, Bay street, Sandridge. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sandridge was the name of the locality at the head of Hobsons Bay where colonial passengers disembarked to journey across the sandy land between there and the Yarra River, Melbourne. It is now known as Port Melbourne.
2 CONT
2 CONT The name Sandridge was given by a surveyor, William Darke, in 1839, after the ridge of sand dunes along the beach. Darke marked a track to the infant Melbourne settlement for travellers and settlers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Apart from Liardet’s Pier Hotel there were few other buildings at Sandridge for several years. In 1849 some township allotments were marked out and Sandridge was defined by six blocks west of the lagoon (now the Esplanade). There 
2 CONC was a government jetty at the end of Bay Street, near the Pier Hotel.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the influx of goldfields, population at Sandridge grew quickly to about 2000 inhabitants in 1854, and its boundaries expanded westwards and northwards to Raglan Street. Agitation began for separation from the Melbourne City 
2 CONC Council, but a hiatus was reached when Sandridge was made a separate ward in 1856 that returned its own councillors. Eventually the agitation was successful and on 13 July 1860, Sandridge borough was proclaimed. The name continued
2 CONC  until 25 January 1884, when the borough was renamed Port Melbourne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sandridge survives as the name of a local lifesaving club and as the name of some local businesses.
