1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Henry ANGEL was born 28/1/1791 (alternative date 28/12/1791) Salisbury, UK  (son of William ANGEL and Mary SHEARAN)
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was baptised 8/1/1792 Woodgreen, UK
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was convicted of highway robbery and sentenced to transportation for life.  He arrived in Sydney Cove on 5/5/1818 on "Neptune".
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1824, he accompanied Hamilton HUME and William HOVELL on their exploration that discovered the River Murray.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry married Mary LEDWIDGE nee BROOKER on 3/9/1834 at Wollongong, NSW and they had 16 children - 8 daughters and 8 sons
2 CONT * Henry 1836
2 CONT * William 1838
2 CONT * Robert 1841
2 CONT * Keturah 1841
2 CONT * Richard 1844
2 CONT * Mary 1847
2 CONT * James 1850
2 CONT * Edward John 1852
2 CONT * Jonathan 1852
2 CONT * Samuel 1853
2 CONT * 6 children died in infancy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry died 17/12/1881 Wagga Wagga
2 CONT ----
2 CONT * http://australianroyalty.net.au/individual.php?pid=I64988&ged=purnellmccord.ged
2 CONT * http://adb.anu.edu.au/biography/angel-henry-2891
2 CONT * http://www.australian-english-genealogy.com/Henry%20Angel.html
2 CONT * http://www.marywadefamily.org/webtrees/individual.php?pid=I05992&ged=marywadefamily.ged
2 CONT * https://convictrecords.com.au/convicts/angel/henry/68477
2 CONT * http://ro.uow.edu.au/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1762&context=ihsbulletin
2 CONT * http://www.smh.com.au/news/obituaries/asia-expert-inspired-many/2008/02/08/1202234160467.html
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hume_and_Hovell_expedition
2 CONT * http://www.shortfamilytree.com/getperson.php?personID=I7620&tree=SHORT
2 CONT * https://shoalhaven.net.au/~borstal/HH%20Tumbarumba%202011.html
2 CONT * http://www.exploreaustralia.net.au/New-South-Wales/The-Murray/Hume-and-Hovell-Walking-Track/Henry-Angel-Trackhead-camping-area
2 CONT * http://www.aussietowns.com.au/town/tumbarumba-nsw
2 CONT * http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=elvaboyle&id=I5889
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ANGEL, HENRY (1791-1881), grazier, was born in Salisbury, England, son of William Angel and his wife Mary, née Shearan. Lacking formal education and a marksman all his life Angel became a skilled farm worker. At 26, with a life se
2 CONC ntence from the Warwick Assizes in July 1817, he was transported in the Neptune and arrived at Sydney on 5 May 1818. In 1824 he was one of six servants assigned to accompany Hamilton Hume and William Hovell on their journey of exp
2 CONC loration in which they discovered the River Murray. Both leaders testified to Angel's ability in managing working horses and cattle and attributed part of their success to his careful planning of transport arrangements. On his ret
2 CONC urn he was rewarded with a pair of bullocks and a ticket-of-leave for the Illawarra district. On 3 September 1834 at a schoolhouse near Wollongong he married the young widow, Mary Ledwidge (b. Hawkesbury River, 1812), daughter of 
2 CONC 'John' Brooker, farmer, and Mary Wade, and in 1839 began buying small sections of farming land in Illawarra.
2 CONT
2 CONT In October 1840 Angel was granted a conditional pardon and soon afterwards, when squatting was rapidly spreading in New South Wales, he and John Rae took up the rights to Uardry station on the saltbush plains of the lower Murrumbi
2 CONC dgee. In 1844 he rented his Illawarra farm and moved with his ever-increasing family to the Riverina. Like most inland stations Uardry was first stocked with cattle, and in the 1840s Angel periodically set off for Sydney, 450 mile
2 CONC s (724 km) distant, with a ton of cheese. On such trips he invariably spent a night with Hamilton Hume near Yass. Despite early difficulties, including trouble with Aboriginals, Angel remained at the Heavenly Plain until the early
2 CONC  1860s when he sold the station leasehold and moved to Spring Vale, in the more settled district near Lake Albert, south of Wagga Wagga, where he lived and worked until his death on 17 December 1881, at the age of 91.
2 CONT
2 CONT With his remarkable energy and endurance he was described by James Gormly as 'one of the most reliable, honest, industrious men … abstemious, persevering and full of resource'. In his will he remarked how hard and long he had work
2 CONC ed to gain his estate of several thousand acres and earnestly besought his children not to mortgage or part with it easily. On his death he left several dwellings, some dozen oddly-named pieces of land varying from 80 (32 ha) to 1
2 CONC 000 acres (405 ha) each and many town lots scattered along the family track from Wollongong to Hay.
2 CONT
2 CONT The fertility of the Angels was a byword in the Wagga Wagga district. Besides two children from her first marriage, Mary Angel bore eight sons and eight daughters to Henry; she died in 1890 leaving 13 children, 90 grandchildren an
2 CONC d 49 great-grandchildren. Angel and his wife were buried in the Church of England section of the Wagga Wagga cemetery. 
